<article class="publication-body article-body">

<section class="answer" id="verdict"><p><strong>Verdict:</strong> Notion is a strong choice for teams that want docs, wikis and databases in one shared workspace. Its page and database model can support many workflows, but it needs clear ownership and setup. Teams that need deeper project controls, full offline access or a ready-made structure may be happier with a more focused tool.</p></section>
<h2 id="what-is-notion">What is Notion?</h2>
<p>Notion is a connected workspace for writing, knowledge management and project information. Its editor uses blocks, so a page can combine text, lists, tables, files, links, embeds and database views. Pages can be nested and linked. Databases store records as pages and add properties such as owner, status, date and priority.</p>
<p>This makes Notion different from a basic note app. A team can write a policy, connect it to a project database and show the related work in a board or calendar view. The same workspace can hold onboarding, meeting notes, product plans, content calendars and customer information. The product is flexible, but the team has to decide how those pieces fit together.</p>
<h2 id="main-strengths">What Notion does well</h2>
<h3 id="flexible-docs">1. Flexible documents and pages</h3>
<p>Notion pages can start as simple notes and grow into structured documents. Blocks make it easy to add headings, checklists, callouts, tables, links and embeds without switching editors. A page can also contain child pages and linked database views. This works well for project briefs, meeting notes, product requirements and internal guidance that need to sit close to related information.</p>
<h3 id="database-views">2. Databases that connect information</h3>
<p>Notion databases are useful when a team needs more than a folder of documents. Each record is a page with properties. Views can show the same records as a table, board, calendar, list, gallery or timeline. Relations and rollups connect records across databases. This gives teams a way to build a content calendar, hiring tracker, product roadmap or customer list without creating a separate app for every process.</p>
<p>The trade-off is that a database needs a good design. Teams should define property names, status values and ownership before building several linked databases. If everyone creates a slightly different version of the same tracker, reporting becomes harder and the workspace becomes difficult to maintain.</p>
<h3 id="wiki-verification">3. Wiki ownership and verification</h3>
<p>Notion's wiki features help teams organize reference information and assign owners. Verified pages can show readers that a source is current. This is useful for policies, onboarding, product documentation and repeatable processes. A wiki still needs a review routine. A verification badge does not replace an owner who checks the page and updates it when the process changes.</p>
<h3 id="project-views">4. Lightweight project management</h3>
<p>Notion supports tasks, subtasks, dependencies, custom properties and multiple database views. A small team can create a task board, a calendar and a timeline from the same database. Project notes can live beside the work, which reduces the gap between a decision and the plan that follows it.</p>
<p>Notion is not a full replacement for every project system. Teams that need detailed workload management, time tracking, sprint reporting or a deep task hierarchy should compare <a href="/compare/notion-vs-clickup/">Notion with ClickUp</a> or another project tool.</p>
<h3 id="connected-work">5. Connected work and integrations</h3>
<p>Notion has a public API, webhooks, connected apps and link previews. The pricing page lists connections to tools such as Slack, GitHub and Google Drive, with more advanced connections on higher plans. Developers can build custom integrations around pages, databases and blocks. This helps a workspace connect to existing tools, but it does not remove the need to manage permissions and integration ownership.</p>
<h3 id="ai-features">6. AI features and agents</h3>
<p>Business and Enterprise include Notion Agent, AI Meeting Notes and Enterprise Search beta. Notion announced an updated agent experience and a new credit system on October 19, 2026. Custom Agents are free to try and then cost US$10 per 1,000 monthly Notion credits. Owners on Business and Enterprise can use the credits dashboard to review consumption. Teams should set a budget and decide which agents can use shared information.</p>
<h3 id="offline-pages">7. Offline pages in desktop and mobile apps</h3>
<p>Notion supports offline pages in its desktop and mobile apps. All plans can download individual pages. Paid plans also auto-download recently visited and favorited pages. This is helpful for reference material, but it is not a full offline workspace. The web browser does not support offline mode, subpages need separate downloads and only the first 50 rows of the first database view are included when a database is downloaded.</p>
<h2 id="pricing-and-value">Notion pricing and value</h2>
<div class="publication-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Plan</th><th>Published price</th><th>Useful difference</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Free</td><td>US$0 per member/month</td><td>Personal use; collaborative blocks limited for 2+ members; 5 MB files; 7-day history.</td></tr><tr><td>Plus</td><td>US$10 per member/month</td><td>5 GB files, 30-day history, custom forms, sites and charts.</td></tr><tr><td>Business</td><td>US$20 per member/month</td><td>Notion AI features, 90-day history, private teamspaces and granular database permissions.</td></tr><tr><td>Enterprise</td><td>Custom</td><td>SCIM, audit logs, domain management and advanced security controls.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>Notion offers monthly and yearly payment modes, with a stated saving of up to 20% for yearly billing. The prices above are the published US rates checked November 8, 2026. A 10-seat Plus workspace is about US$100 per month at the displayed per-member rate; Business is about US$200. These examples exclude tax, regional changes, custom domains and extra AI credits. Review the <a href="/notion/pricing/">Notion pricing guide</a> before budgeting.</p>
<p>Free and Plus list a limited AI trial. Business and Enterprise include Notion AI features. Custom Agents use credits after the free trial, and the pricing page lists US$10 per 1,000 monthly credits. The new credit system took effect for new Business and Enterprise workspaces on October 19, 2026, and existing workspaces on November 2. Older descriptions of a separate AI add-on may not match the current system.</p>
<h2 id="limitations">Where Notion needs care</h2>
<ul><li><strong>Workspace design takes time.</strong> Teams need naming rules, database owners, status definitions and a plan for old pages.</li><li><strong>Free is not unlimited for a team.</strong> Collaborative blocks are limited for workspaces with two or more members, and file uploads are capped at 5 MB each.</li><li><strong>Offline support is partial.</strong> It works in desktop and mobile apps, not the browser. Pages and subpages need downloads, and database downloads have a row limit.</li><li><strong>Exports are useful but not a full restore.</strong> Workspace export supports HTML, Markdown and CSV, with uploaded files. Pages the exporter cannot access or content excluded by teamspace settings may be missing. Reuploading the export does not recreate the original workspace.</li><li><strong>AI usage needs a budget.</strong> Business and Enterprise include AI features, but Custom Agents use credits. Track use instead of assuming unlimited agent runs.</li><li><strong>Project controls may not be deep enough.</strong> Teams that depend on time tracking, detailed workload views or sprint reporting may need a dedicated project system.</li></ul>
<h2 id="setup-maintenance">Setup and maintenance affect the value</h2>
<p>Notion's editor is easy to start using, but a shared workspace needs a few decisions. Pick a page naming pattern, decide which databases are shared, assign owners to reference pages and agree on status names. Use templates for repeat work, but do not create a template for every possible case. Too many templates can make it harder to know which one is current.</p>
<p>Search is most useful when people use consistent titles and properties. If the same policy has several copies, users may open the wrong version. Keep one canonical page and link to it from projects. Add a review date to policies and onboarding pages. When a page is no longer useful, archive it or move it to a clearly named historical area rather than leaving it in the main navigation.</p>
<h2 id="security-administration">Security and administration</h2>
<p>Notion offers page sharing, teamspaces, permission groups and plan-based security features. Business includes private teamspaces, SAML SSO and granular database permissions. Enterprise adds SCIM, audit logs, domain management and other controls. These features are useful only when someone owns access reviews. Make a list of sensitive pages, identify their owners and check guest access after a project or contract ends.</p>
<p>For AI use, decide which connected apps and databases an agent can access. Notion's October 2026 release notes describe the new credit system and updated agent features. Owners can review usage in the Notion AI dashboard. A team should start with a small agent task, set an owner and review the output before allowing a workflow to update shared records automatically.</p>
<h2 id="who-should-use-notion">Who should use Notion?</h2>
<p>Notion is a good fit for teams that write and share a lot of internal information. It suits product teams, marketers, researchers, founders and operations staff who want docs and databases connected. It is also useful for a solo user who wants a linked knowledge base and can keep the structure simple.</p>
<p>It is less suitable when a team wants a complete project management system without setup, a full offline local-file workflow or strict data portability with a one-click restore. If those are the main needs, compare a focused alternative before moving important work.</p>
<h2 id="notion-alternatives">Notion alternatives to compare</h2>
<p><a href="/notion/alternatives/">The Notion alternatives guide</a> compares ClickUp, Confluence, Slab, Nuclino, Obsidian, Anytype and Taskade. ClickUp suits task-first project teams, Slab suits a focused company wiki, Obsidian suits local Markdown notes, and Anytype is worth a look when local-first storage is a priority. None is a perfect copy of Notion, so choose by the work you need to replace.</p>
<h2 id="review-methodology">Review methodology</h2>
<p>This review is based on Notion's official product pages, pricing, help documentation and release notes checked on November 8, 2026. It does not claim hands-on testing, measured performance, user interviews or a customer rating. The recommendations are editorial judgments based on the documented features and limits. Prices are public US list prices and may change with billing mode, region, tax and seat count.</p>
<h2 id="faq">Common questions</h2><h3>Is Notion worth using?</h3><p>Notion is worth considering when a team wants docs, wikis, databases and project context in one place. It is less suitable when the main need is advanced project controls, full offline browser use or a low-maintenance structure that needs little setup.</p>
<h3>Is Notion free?</h3><p>Yes. The Free plan costs $0 per member per month. It is generous for an individual, but collaborative blocks are limited for workspaces with two or more members.</p>
<h3>Does Notion work offline?</h3><p>Notion supports downloaded pages in its desktop and mobile apps. Paid plans also auto-download recently visited and favorited pages. The web browser does not support the offline feature.</p>
<h3>Does Notion include AI in every plan?</h3><p>No. Free and Plus include a limited trial. Business and Enterprise include Notion AI features. Custom Agents use a separate Notion credit system.</p>
<h3>Can you export a Notion workspace?</h3><p>You can export workspace pages as HTML or Markdown and databases as CSV, with uploaded files. Pages you cannot access and content excluded by teamspace settings may be left out, and an export cannot instantly recreate the workspace.</p>
